---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

// V-705 — migration guide for teams coming from Browserless.
//
// W228.A — accuracy pass. The previous revision claimed:
//   - client.sessions.start({target_url, script}) — start() doesn't exist
//   - script body field on session create — not part of the schema
//   - client.sessions.waitUntilTerminal() / getResult() — don't exist
//   - profile_id body field on session create — not part of the schema
//   - "recordings work by default" — recordings aren't shipped (V-540)
// All of those have been removed in favour of the real action-based
// surface (navigate/interact/wait/capture). Watched by
// apps/server/tests/unit/migration-from-browserless-doc-parity.test.ts.
---

<BaseLayout title="Migrate from Browserless — Driftstack" description="A field guide for porting a Browserless workload onto Driftstack: surface mapping, session lifecycle, profiles, and tier picks.">
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-12 prose prose-slate dark:prose-invert">
    <h1>Migrate from Browserless</h1>

    <p class="lead">
      You have an existing Browserless deployment (cloud or
      self-hosted) and want to evaluate Driftstack. This page maps
      the two surfaces so you can scope the port without surprises.
      Driftstack is action-based, not script-passthrough: every
      browser step is an HTTP call, and there is no
      <code>/function</code> equivalent that runs arbitrary JS
      server-side.
    </p>

    <h2>Surface comparison</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Browserless</th><th>Driftstack</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Connect to <code>wss://chrome.browserless.io?token=…</code> + run Puppeteer/Playwright over CDP</td>
          <td>POST <code>/v1/sessions</code> to create a session,
            then drive it with action endpoints
            (<code>navigate</code>, <code>interact</code>,
            <code>wait</code>, <code>capture</code>). No CDP
            passthrough.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>/function</code> endpoint (post a JS body, get JSON back)</td>
          <td>No direct equivalent. Express the steps as discrete
            actions; if you need a richer extraction primitive,
            email us.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>/screenshot</code> + <code>/pdf</code></td>
          <td><code>POST /v1/sessions/:id/capture</code> with
            <code>kind=screenshot</code>, <code>dom_snapshot</code>,
            or <code>pdf</code>. Returns inline base64 bytes — no
            presigned URL.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Concurrency-limit token (manual)</td>
          <td>Tier-driven concurrency cap; over-cap returns
            <code>429</code> with the
            <code>concurrency-limit</code> RFC 7807 type.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Stealth plugin / fingerprint flags</td>
          <td>Profile archetypes (V-281) + per-account fingerprint
            state.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>The first port: replace the WebSocket</h2>
    <p>
      Most Browserless integrations consist of a Puppeteer script
      pointed at <code>wss://chrome.browserless.io</code> instead of
      <code>puppeteer.launch()</code>. In Driftstack you express the
      same flow as discrete actions:
    </p>

    <pre><code>{`// Browserless style
const browser = await puppeteer.connect({
  browserWSEndpoint: \`wss://chrome.browserless.io?token=\${TOKEN}\`,
});
const page = await browser.newPage();
await page.goto('https://example.com');
const title = await page.title();
await browser.close();
return title;`}</code></pre>

    <pre><code>{`// Driftstack equivalent (TypeScript SDK)
import { Driftstack } from '@driftstack/sdk';

const client = new Driftstack({ apiKey: process.env.DRIFTSTACK_API_KEY! });

const session = await client.sessions.create({ archetype: 'default' });
await client.sessions.navigate(session.id, { url: 'https://example.com' });
const state = await client.sessions.getState(session.id);
// state.url is the resolved URL, state.title is the page title.
const title = state.title;
await client.sessions.destroy(session.id);
console.log(title);`}</code></pre>

    <p>
      No server-side JS. Each step is an HTTP round-trip, so a
      typical login + scrape is a handful of POSTs followed by a
      <code>GET /v1/sessions/&lt;id&gt;/state</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>Profile persistence: the differentiator</h2>
    <p>
      Browserless gives you a fresh browser per request unless you
      manage your own cookie jar. Driftstack profiles (V-312)
      persist cookies / localStorage / IndexedDB on the server in
      the WebKit driver layer. Mint a profile via the Profiles API
      and pin its archetype; future sessions can share its state via
      a snapshot-restore flow (the customer-facing API does not pin
      a profile to a specific session at create time today — see
      <a href="/docs/profiles">/docs/profiles</a> for the current
      shape).
    </p>

    <pre><code>{`const profile = await client.profiles.create({
  name: 'evergreen-scraper',
  archetype: 'default',
});

// Drive a login flow.
const s = await client.sessions.create({ archetype: 'default' });
await client.sessions.navigate(s.id, { url: 'https://example.com/login' });
await client.sessions.interact(s.id, {
  action: 'type', selector: '#email', value: 'bot@you.dev',
});
// …
await client.sessions.destroy(s.id);

// Capture the post-login state into the profile for replay later.
await client.profiles.captureSnapshot(profile.id, {
  label: 'logged-in-baseline',
});`}</code></pre>

    <h2>Recordings</h2>
    <p>
      Browserless gives you a one-frame PDF or PNG. Driftstack
      session recordings are on the
      <a href="/docs/recordings">roadmap</a> (V-540) but not shipped
      today. Until they land, drive observation with per-step
      <code>capture</code> calls.
    </p>

    <h2>Tier picks for typical Browserless workloads</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Light scraping (≤ 5 concurrent)</strong> — <a href="/pricing">api_starter</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Mid-volume scrapers</strong> — api_builder.</li>
      <li><strong>QA / E2E test infrastructure</strong> — api_scale.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Things Driftstack does NOT do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Raw WebSocket / CDP passthrough. The action endpoints are
        the contract; arbitrary CDP messages are blocked on purpose
        to keep the per-request cost predictable.</li>
      <li>Server-side JS execution. There is no
        <code>/function</code>-style endpoint; express your flow as
        actions or do the extraction client-side off the
        <code>state</code> response.</li>
      <li>Self-hosted on-prem. Cloud only today.</li>
      <li>Browser type selection. WebKit only. Chrome + Firefox are
        on the roadmap.</li>
      <li>Session recordings — see
        <a href="/docs/recordings">/docs/recordings</a> for status.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Migration checklist</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Inventory your existing Browserless calls — count
        <code>/function</code> vs <code>/screenshot</code> vs raw
        CDP.</li>
      <li>If <code>/function</code> bodies do non-trivial JS
        evaluation or raw CDP &gt; 10% of calls: get in touch
        before starting; the action-based surface may not cover
        you yet.</li>
      <li>Port one job. The <a href="/docs/sdk-typescript">TypeScript
        SDK</a> or <a href="/docs/sdk-python">Python SDK</a> takes
        a couple of hours per job.</li>
      <li>Set up <a href="/docs/webhooks">webhooks</a> for
        session-completed events — replace your polling loops.</li>
      <li>Wire <a href="/docs/cost-monitoring">cost monitoring</a>
        alerts before flipping production traffic.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Need help porting?</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="mailto:developers@driftstack.dev">developers@driftstack.dev</a>.
      Bring your <code>/function</code> bodies; we'll scope a tier
      + grant a credit to try it.
    </p>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
